---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B2: When a frame is required but none is free, some frame must be
>> evicted.  Describe your code for choosing a frame to evict.

The frame eviction process utilizes the "clock algorithm". We keep a pointer
that circles the frame_list element by element for exaclty two cycles.
If the pointer meets an "accessed page", it sets it to "unaccessed" but skips
(aka giving second chance). However, if it sees any unaccessed page when it
circles back, it would evict that page. Note that any pinned page is skipped.
For the page that's picked for eviction, it either goes to swap or do nothing
if it's in file system.


>> B3: When a process P obtains a frame that was previously used by a
>> process Q, how do you adjust the page table (and any other data
>> structures) to reflect the frame Q no longer has?

Before reuse, the frame’s previous page table mapping is removed via 
pagedir_clear_page (marking it as not present and clear the TLB too), 
which CPU will no longer able to translate virtual address to that evicted
frame without causing a page fault. It also removes that entry from the 
global frame list.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B5: Explain the basics of your VM synchronization design.  In
>> particular, explain how it prevents deadlock.  (Refer to the
>> textbook for an explanation of the necessary conditions for
>> deadlock.)


The necessary conditions for deadlock are:
1. Mutual exclusion - threads claim exclusive access to resources
2. Hold and wait - threads hold a resource and wait for another
3. No preemption - resources cannot be forcibly removed from a thread
4. Circular wait - threads form a circular chain of waiting resources

The design that we chose is a lock based design to handle synchronization. We utilize a global frame table lock 
to protect the frame table from race conditions. Additionally, we use page locks
for each SPT entry to protect individual pages and allow operations on different pages to happen 
in parallel. We address the necessary conditions for deadlock by:

* only using non-blocking lock aquisition (lock_try_acquire) in our clock algorithm to avoid holding and waiting
* establishing a global order of lock aquisition (frame table first, then SPT, then swap table) 
* temporary releasing the lock when waiting for I/O operations to complete in the frame eviction process
* pinning frames to avoid eviction while a process is holding a page lock
* releasing locks when circular dependecies could occur (e.g. when a process is evicting a page that another process is waiting for)


>> B6: A page fault in process P can cause another process Q's frame
>> to be evicted.  How do you ensure that Q cannot access or modify
>> the page during the eviction process?  How do you avoid a race
>> between P evicting Q's frame and Q faulting the page back in?

We implemented mechanisms to ensure safe eviction. First, when we start the process to evict Q's frame,
we clear Q's page directory to prevent Q from accessing the page, part of which includes invalidating TLB entries 
for the mapping. This makes it so that Q will not have access to the page without causing a page fault.
We use page locks, so that P must acquire the lock for Q's SPT entry before evicting Q's frame.
We atomically update the SPT entry while the page lock is held. Since P holds both the frame 
table lock and the page lock, this prevents undesired access to the frame. Once the page is evicted,
the frame entry is removed from the frame table and Q will need to allocate a new frame if it wants to 
fault in the page again. These mechanisms ensure that Q can not access or modify the page during
the eviction process, preventing race conditions.

>> B7: Suppose a page fault in process P causes a page to be read from
>> the file system or swap.  How do you ensure that a second process Q
>> cannot interfere by e.g. attempting to evict the frame while it is
>> still being read in?

We handle this by pinning the frame as soon as it gets allocated. This happens in the load_page() function.
When the evicttion process occurs, we explicitly check if a frame is pinned. If it is, we skip it and don't consider it for eviction.
Once the frame is fully loaded into memory and the page table is updated, we unpin it. This ensures
the frame is not evicted while being loaded.


>> B8: Explain how you handle access to paged-out pages that occur
>> during system calls.  Do you use page faults to bring in pages (as
>> in user programs), or do you have a mechanism for "locking" frames
>> into physical memory, or do you use some other design?  How do you
>> gracefully handle attempted accesses to invalid virtual addresses?

We handle this by validating all pages before accessing them and using the page fault handler to load the page. 
We use frame pinning to prevent eviction during the system call and then unpin all frames when the system call 
is complete. This way, we can ensure all pages required by the system call are loaded into memory before being 
used. When attempting to access an invalid virtual address, a graceful failure occurs. We return an error code and
ensure partial writes do not occur. The virtual address is not added to the page table. We make sure to 
properly validate the address before accessing it, by checking the address range. We also check if the
address exists in the page table. 

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: A single lock for the whole VM system would make
>> synchronization easy, but limit parallelism.  On the other hand,
>> using many locks complicates synchronization and raises the
>> possibility for deadlock but allows for high parallelism.  Explain
>> where your design falls along this continuum and why you chose to
>> design it this way.

We chose to implement a sort of hierarchical locking design. We use both a global lock and page specific locks.
The global lock is used to protect the frame table and the swap table from race conditions. The page specific locks
protect the STP entries from race conditions. The file system lock protects the file I/O operations from race conditions.

We chose this type of design because it employs a moderate approach that balances parallelism and synchronization.
This also allows for a manageable amount of complexity with very well-defined locks that have clear purposes. 


